WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.644
Hi, I'm Parsley!

00:00:02.644 --> 00:00:07.606
When poses drift, feed-forward Gaussian splatting fails quietly: input views look

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:09.411
fine, new views don't.

00:00:09.411 --> 00:00:10.765
Here's VoxelTTO's fix.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.809
Anchor the Gaussians to voxels, not pixels.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:19.066
Then, per scene, fit small adapters until the frozen backbone's cameras

00:00:19.066 --> 00:00:20.615
agree with your poses.

00:00:21.942 --> 00:00:24.594
Most models make one Gaussian per pixel.

00:00:24.594 --> 00:00:29.899
Neighbouring views see the same surface, so it gets overlapping Gaussians: floaters and shells.

00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:35.108
VoxelTTO fuses the backbone's tokens into one global voxel grid, encodes it

00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:39.498
with a sparse 3D U-Net, and splits each voxel into Gaussians.

00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:45.476
Images climb the frozen backbone, which also decodes a camera for every view.

00:00:45.896 --> 00:00:50.498
Adapters go on the query, key and value projections of layers thirteen to forty.

00:00:50.498 --> 00:00:52.142
The first twelve get nothing.

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:54.053
The loss isn't photometric.

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:57.780
It compares the decoded cameras with the poses you supplied.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.961
Twenty steps bend the backbone's attention until its cameras agree.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:04.593
The Gaussians move because the features move.

00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:09.460
Here are both loops, in the paper's own figure.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.100
Snowflakes mark what never moves.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.035
At test time, the orange backward path runs from the pose loss

00:01:15.035 --> 00:01:16.675
into the LoRA modules only.

00:01:17.095 --> 00:01:21.768
At training time, it reaches only the voxel branch below, which builds the Gaussians.

00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:26.847
On Tanks and Temples, rotation error falls about six times, and the

00:01:26.847 --> 00:01:31.325
view gap shrinks from nine point oh three decibels to one point six six.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.985
The fit doesn't make the model better.

00:01:34.985 --> 00:01:38.035
It decides whose coordinate frame wins, and always picks yours.

00:01:38.035 --> 00:01:41.390
If your poses are wrong, it drags the scene onto them.

00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:47.005
Voxels instead of pixels, adapters fitted to your poses, and a trade

00:01:47.005 --> 00:01:49.141
worth checking against your own cameras.

00:01:49.561 --> 00:01:51.713
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:51.713 --> 00:01:52.328
I'm Parsley.

00:01:52.328 --> 00:01:52.636
Bye!
